X-SOURCE-ID:bohemian
SUMMARY:Si Pudiera Quedarme / If I Could Stay
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260927T163000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260927T183000
UID:260923mvBMKknpLUOGUP4Aij8EUA
DESCRIPTION:Film Screening & Conversation with Award-Winning Filmmaker The
 o Rigby and a Special Guest\n\nWhat does it mean to fight for the right to
  stay home with your family? \n\n\n\nSunday\, September 27\, 4:30 -6:30 pm
 \n\nPetaluma Historical Library & Museum\n\n20 4th Street \\(Corner of 4th
  & B Street\\)\n\nSuggested donation $5-$10. No one will be turned away fo
 r lack of funds.\n\nReserve Here\n\nJoin award-winning filmmaker Theo Rigb
 y for a screening of Si Pudiera Quedarme / If I Could Stay\, followed by
  a conversation with Rigby and a special guest.\n\nFilmed over five years\
 , Si Pudiera Quedarme is an intimate and moving portrait of Jeanette an
 d Ingrid\, two undocumented Latinx mothers who enter sanctuary in Bay Area
  churches rather than face deportation and separation from their families.
  \n\nAs the women navigate extraordinary uncertainty\, they organize\, adv
 ocate\, and build relationships with faith communities standing alongside 
 them. Their stories illuminate the courage of families confronting the imm
 igration system while exploring questions of sanctuary\, allyship\, power\
 , faith\, and what it means to truly belong. \n\nAt the center of the film
  are Jeanette and Ingrid themselves—not simply as people facing deportat
 ion\, but as leaders claiming their voices and mobilizing communities arou
 nd them. Their journeys offer a powerful story of resilience\, family\, an
 d the determination to create change. \n\nFollowing the approximately one-
 hour film\, stay for a conversation and audience Q&A with filmmaker Theo 
 Rigby and a special guest. \n\nAbout Theo Rigby\n\n \n\nTheo Rigby is an
  award-winning San Francisco-based director\, cinematographer\, and intera
 ctive storyteller whose work has focused on the immigrant experience in th
 e United States for more than a decade. He is the founder of iNation Media
  and the creator of projects including Waking Dream and Immigrant Natio
 n. His films have been featured by The New York Times\, broadcast nationa
 lly on PBS’s POV\, and screened at Ellis Island\, the New York Film Fes
 tival\, and Cannes Film Festival. His film Sin País \\(Without Country\\
 ) received a Student Academy Award. Rigby holds an MFA in Documentary Fil
 m from Stanford University and previously worked as an award-winning photo
 journalist. \n\nThis event is presented in conjunction with Belongings: W
 hat We Bring\, What We Leave Behind\, on view September 4–October 4\, 2
 026\, at the Petaluma Historical Library & Museum. The exhibition explores
  migration\, memory\, and belonging through the treasured objects and pers
 onal stories that connect us across generations. \n\nBelongings: What We B
 ring\, What We Leave Behind is made possible through the generous support
  of California Humanities through its Connecting California grant prog
 ram. We are also deeply grateful to our community partners and sponsors\, 
 including the North Bay Asian American Pacific Islander Coalition\, Sono
 ma County Japanese American Citizens League\, Petaluma Arts Center\, Lif
 e on Art\, Zimbabwe Cultural Exchange Project of California\, El Día de
  los Muertos Petaluma\, Bolca Avia\, Chelsea Antiques\, Café Puente\,
  Rustic Bakery\, Quality Inn Petaluma\, and all of the organizations\, v
 olunteers\, artists\, and community members whose collaboration has brough
 t this exhibition to life.
LOCATION:Petaluma Historical Library and Museum
URL:https://www.petalumamuseum.com/calendar-event/si-pudiera-quedarme-if-i
 -could-stay/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
